#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616175 :question/problem "![](images/42/420e97ad7d5e83c368138eee90d180b40a9ec946c4336d561c428e25dcb8205d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1$\n\nII. $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{4}{3}x$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, we can choose $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(3,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/ff/ffd1eea07781890c1dd539e2e36f796d9b18bb4b05b33a903279b88b329f9082.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1.$ \nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =\\frac{3}{4}(0)+1 \\\\ 4 & =1\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{4}x+1$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =4-\\frac{4}{3}(0) \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =4-\\frac{4}{3}(3) \\\\ 0 & =0\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{4}{3}x.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $B(3,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0) & =\\frac{4}{3}(3) \\\\ 0 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{4}{3}x$ is *not* an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=4-\\frac{4}{3}x,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616186 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,-3).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=6.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6)-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(6,0).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3.$\n\n![](images/5c/5c09dcad81445121f3ded344b6346f9375588166f8a80204cb77043da43ca6d0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616191 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-2x+3?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+3 \\\\  & =-2(0)+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is$(0,3).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be  $x=2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+3 \\\\  & =-2(2)+3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-2x+3.$\n\n![](images/7f/7fe451811427028f9475bbcdfc24915848e0e627f19102604e253b44904c1b07.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616195 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=1-2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=1-2x.$\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=1-2(0)=1$;\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=1-2(1)=-1$;\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=1-2(2)=-3$;\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=1-2(3)=-5.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-5$ |\n\nPlotting these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane would look like this: \n\n![](images/83/839a5bfb3fe69ea3c02f24b4ad428b1db9b0c58e7cd043b370a19b2ff332347a.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line $y=1-2x.$\n\n![](images/49/497d1c67fb3a322ae402f6dfa69c8b069d91177ddef6a5847e6dbee4cb93f995.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616198 :question/problem "![](images/22/2223ef6e2f8d208453646c3698009cc30271ca5725409c859138bede98985e29.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-3x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,7)$, $B(4,3)$ and $C(2,-5).$ \n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+1 \\\\ (7) & =-3(-2)+1 \\\\ 7 & =7\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(4,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+1 \\\\ (3) & =-3(4)+1 \\\\ 3 & =-11\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+1 \\\\ (-5) & =-3(2)+1 \\\\ -5 & =-5\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $B$ does not lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616204 :question/problem "![](images/53/53cfc55c22f9fb48802ff09133edd1fb0f406c00619df97cbb3a1f9737376c24.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(1,3),B(3,6)$ and $C(5,3).$ \n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(1,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+3 \\\\ (3) & =(1)+3 \\\\ 3 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(3,6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+3 \\\\ (6) & =(3)+3 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(5,3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+3 \\\\ (3) & =(5)+3 \\\\ 3 & =8\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $B$ lies on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616210 :question/problem "![](images/d5/d58e330c54284f7f3df8b96976741558d42728eae2b3944315a9f6fbb8415054.png)\n\nIdentify the points that **DO NOT** lie on the line $y=-10x-23?$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-2,-3)$, $B(1,-3)$ and $C(3,-3).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-2,-3)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-10x-23 \\\\ (-3) & =-10(-2)-23 \\\\ -3 & =-3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-10x-23 \\\\ (-3) & =-10(1)-23 \\\\ -3 & =-33\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(3,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-10x-23 \\\\ (-3) & =-10(3)-23 \\\\ -3 & =-53\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $B$ and $C$ do not lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616214 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+2?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}x+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0)+2 \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=6.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}x+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(6)+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(6,4).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+2.$\n\n![](images/6a/6ac6477bf097c6691715a4e38ee6bb400ede800a887d627b84c6ac45a12c4d43.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616295 :question/problem "Let $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨-3,11,2⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨5,10,-8⟩.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{u}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{u}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{v} & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y}+{u}_{z}\\cdot {v}_{z} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 5+11\\cdot 10+2\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-15+110-16 \\\\  & =79\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616296 :question/problem "If $a = ⟨ - 9, 11, -9⟩$ and $b = ⟨-3, -10,3⟩$, then $a \\cdot b =$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =(-9)\\cdot (-3)+11\\cdot (-10)+(-9)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27-110-27 \\\\  & =-110\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616299 :question/problem "If $a = ⟨ - 1, -3, -9⟩$ and $b = ⟨-3, 9,-7⟩$, then $a \\cdot b =$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 9+(-9)\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =3-27+63 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616305 :question/problem "If $v=⟨3,-8,0,-3⟩$ and $w=⟨0,9,0,0⟩$, then $v\\cdot w=$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot w & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{v}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{v}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{v}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0+(-8)\\cdot 9+0\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0-72+0+0 \\\\  & =-72\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616306 :question/problem "If $v=⟨12,11,6,3⟩$ and $w=⟨-2,-8,-2,-5⟩$, then $v\\cdot w=$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot w & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{v}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{v}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{v}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-2)+11\\cdot (-8)+6\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-24-88-12-15 \\\\  & =-139\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616327 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-9-4x)^{6}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{6},\\,g=-9-4x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6g^{5},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=6g^{5}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  &=-24(-9-4x)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616329 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(3 x - 2)^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{3},\\,g=3 x - 2\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3g^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=3g^{2}\\cdot (3) \\\\  &=9(3 x - 2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616946 :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616955 :question/problem "![](images/40/402ba8432eca5bd5e15839d84c3ea58baf5500afa653e903b2abdcabb3eea92e.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $6$ centered at $(-2,5)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,5).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $6$ centered at the point $(-2,5).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616967 :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $N(1,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $N$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =\\frac{2(1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $N(1,1)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 2x & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $N(1,1)$ is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616977 :question/problem "![](images/3d/3db3257b5c00e722ea71f9973dc47994c01068ec29e95c045835e803ab3720b2.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=5.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/a8/a8fc93176ea43d63a918e58ac43d545f620c64c9ed3c2b85589856125718d9f3.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $[\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616978 :question/problem "![](images/24/244e3cebb0eb0607773af1696c31961e646977ecf2a52308e9ecdc8ae81994a5.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $11$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(11,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=11.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=11\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617022 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(6\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =36{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(6\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =36{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =36{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +36{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =36({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =36(1) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617185 :question/problem "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3x\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $u⊥v$ and $x<0$, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $u⊥v$ ($u$ and $v$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v &={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  &=2\\cdot 3+x\\cdot (-3x) \\\\  &=6-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v &=0 \\\\ 6-3{x}^{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}^{2} &=-6 \\\\ {x}^{2} &=2 \\\\ x &=\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $x<0.$ Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617186 :question/problem "Let $g=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -3{r}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $h=\\begin{bmatrix}2{r}^{2} \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $g⊥h$ and $r>0$, find the value of $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h &={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  &=4\\cdot 2{r}^{2}+8\\cdot 4+(-3{r}^{2})\\cdot 4 \\\\  &=8r^{2}+32-12r^{2} \\\\  &=-4{r}^{2}+32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h &=0 \\\\ -4{r}^{2}+32 &=0 \\\\ -4{r}^{2} &=-32 \\\\ {r}^{2} &=8 \\\\ r &=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $r>0.$ Therefore, $r=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617202 :question/problem "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}-3x \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $u⊥v$ and $x>0$, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $u⊥v$ ($u$ and $v$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v &={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  &=(-3x)\\cdot x+9\\cdot 4 \\\\  &=36-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v &=0 \\\\ 36-3{x}^{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}^{2} &=-36 \\\\ {x}^{2} &=12 \\\\ x &=\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $x>0.$ Therefore, $x=2\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617203 :question/problem "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $u⊥v$ and $a>0$, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $u⊥v$ ($u$ and $v$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v &={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y}+{u}_{z}\\cdot {v}_{z} \\\\  &=a\\cdot a+2\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  &={a}^{2}-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v &=0 \\\\ {a}^{2}-12 &=0 \\\\ {a}^{2} &=12 \\\\ a &=\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $a>0.$ Therefore, $a=2\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617206 :question/problem "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $u⊥v$ and $a<0$, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $u⊥v$ ($u$ and $v$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v &={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y}+{u}_{z}\\cdot {v}_{z} \\\\  &=(-3)\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot (-2)+a\\cdot a \\\\  &={a}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v &=0 \\\\ {a}^{2}-2 &=0 \\\\ {a}^{2} &=2 \\\\ a &=\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $a<0.$ Therefore, $a=-\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617785 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+2y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x+2y)^{4}-y=0$ and  the line $y=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+2y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+2y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2y)^{4}-y &=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{4}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{4} \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm 1 \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{4}\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/21/21a418b848dc981a9490ca72038a077205d79cf152b00581c5bc6eb7f48269a9.png)\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{4}}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}v{|}_{{u}^{4}}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{4})du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617805 :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x+{30}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=8.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{8}={45}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617817 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\ny=3x,\\,y=3x-4,\\,x=y-1,\\,x=y+2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n3x-y=0,\\,3x-y=4,\\,x-y=-1,\\,x-y=2\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=3x-y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=3x-y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3x-y=0\\,⟹\\,u=0 \\\\  & 3x-y=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x-y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x-y=-1\\,⟹\\,v=-1 \\\\  & x-y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/ff/ff0564b19ac1a55b0bc5d50a8b93bf0a6196a92c388d95a82bccf74039243e05.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3+1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot (2-(-1)) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617824 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the half-ellipsoid $z=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=⟨z,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩,$ the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-ellipsoid $z=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the cylinder. One such surface is our half-ellipsoid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{-4x}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{-y}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨z,\\,0,\\,0⟩{|}_{z=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{0}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{4x}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)-0\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\right)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/eae72066363dd2c749f85fcc1568c15d65d193af83b9185ab084aef9a38db045.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4x\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617841 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{x},\\,y=\\frac{5}{x},\\,x={y}^{2},\\,x=4{y}^{2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=2,\\,xy=5,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=4\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & xy=5\\,⟹\\,u=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=4\\,⟹\\,v=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/ff/ff8f8b6af1d1a81a0c7f40b29e92351431a45f67ebc057fd39acd2cd2280f48b.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617844 :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x+y)^{2}-x=0$ and the line $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-x &=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x &=2\\,⟹\\,v=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{2} \\\\ v &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},2).$ \n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,{u}^{2}\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/34/349486642eca07c8f1a619cb567dcb578c39a5ff024067a0910301039f3ae1d9.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(-1)^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}}^{2}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{{u}^{2}}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617933 :question/problem "![](images/91/91f73d4130edb919d753352e6fb1d1a013709e28d62cdf30a0c8fc5b8fdf7e4c.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x-2$." :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(1,2)$, $B(-2,-6)$, $C(-4,-8)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(1,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x-2 \\\\ (2) &= 2(1)-2 \\\\ 2 &= 0 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-2,-6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x-2 \\\\ (-6) &= 2(-2)-2 \\\\ -6 &= -6 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-4,-8)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x-2 \\\\ (-8) &= 2(-4)-2 \\\\ -8 &= -10 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $B$ lies on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617934 :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b88f738026aa7528e4e25c5c36ebd48c7c9f6dd497478ae746555ea790bcf5f.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x-3$." :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(3,3)$, $B(0,-5)$, $C(-1,-5)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(3,3)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x-3 \\\\ (3) &= 2(3)-3 \\\\ 3 &= 3 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,-5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x-3 \\\\ (-5) &= 2(0)-3 \\\\ -5 &= -3 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(-1,-5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x-3 \\\\ (-5) &= 2(-1)-3 \\\\ -5 &= -5 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617943 :question/problem "![](images/1b/1bb11ff96613977acc678334ea9ffc163acc79fb771e36ab179392ca964aac59.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=x$." :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-3,-3)$, $B(4,1)$, $C(2,2)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-3,-3)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x \\\\ (-3) &= (-3) \\\\ -3 &= -3 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(4,1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x \\\\ (1) &= (4) \\\\ 1 &= 4 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x \\\\ (2) &= (2) \\\\ 2 &= 2 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617948 :question/problem "![](images/15/1527d66e141103edd1a772b7cb6431f795ca58b02ad2ff3ba73b9f7cf6f9f63e.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x+5$." :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-4,-4)$, $B(0,5)$, $C(2,9)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-4,-4)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+5 \\\\ (-4) &= 2(-4)+5 \\\\ -4 &= -3 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,5)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+5 \\\\ (5) &= 2(0)+5 \\\\ 5 &= 5 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,9)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x+5 \\\\ (9) &= 2(2)+5 \\\\ 9 &= 9 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $B$ and $C$ lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617964 :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9af9b389b7306f786a714929e92697313d5d335c701a136611992c34a456798.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=x-4$." :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,-4)$, $B(3,-1)$, $C(2,-3)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,-4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x-4 \\\\ (-4) &= (0)-4 \\\\ -4 &= -4 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(3,-1)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x-4 \\\\ (-1) &= (3)-4 \\\\ -1 &= -1 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-3)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= x-4 \\\\ (-3) &= (2)-4 \\\\ -3 &= -2 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does not lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $B$ lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618013 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2x+1.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2x+1.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2(0)+1=1,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=2(1)+1=3,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2(2)+1=5,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=2(3)+1=7.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/35/35483254ead1f56d168138c1cf010a4f95112490b85113c01e6efcd3e5f3ee3e.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=2x+1.$\n\n![](images/ae/aed04c3845df13431f4d64cd9587070ae4c2bc15e1802d00ca8fdc170f7987b2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618023 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=-x.$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=-x.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=-(2)=-2,$\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=-(3)=-3,$\n\n- if $x=4$ then $y=-(4)=-4,$ and\n\n- if $x=5$ then $y=-(5)=-5.$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-5$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d288b6b97a573fd2a526dad6241a85347a6e2071212340131d11e9d0f5e012f5.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=-x.$\n\n![](images/30/30af7776308a0bf8a76cbbfcededfe11b145f29c1edc0855c2f3c5339b201934.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618040 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2x+2.$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2x+2.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=2(1)+2=4,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2(2)+2=6,$\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=2(3)+2=8,$ and\n\n- if $x=4$ then $y=2(4)+2=10.$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/10/107054b3b3a3a52642416caa394926fb9e4ea8d4ca6906b45899a56fd771672b.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=2x+2.$\n\n![](images/26/26d63be2a0b276a05a9248caedfa06311cb473454c43559d6d51825e9f27d11b.png)"}]}
